@database A314Mount.guide
@$VER: A314Mount.guide 1.0 (07.06.2026)
@author Laine Jones
@wordwrap

@node Main "A314Mount 0.1"

  @{b}A314Mount@{ub} - mount ADF and HDF images on the A314 disk drives

  Insert and eject ADF (floppy) and HDF (hard-disk) images in the A314's
  emulated drives PD0: to PD3:, straight from the Amiga - no more telnetting
  into the Pi to swap disks.

    @{" Introduction   " link Intro}
    @{" Requirements   " link Reqs}
    @{" Installation   " link Install}
    @{" CLI usage      " link CLI}
    @{" GUI usage      " link GUI}
    @{" ADF vs HDF     " link HDF}
    @{" Troubleshooting" link Trouble}

  Ships as a Shell command (A314Mount) and a GadTools GUI (A314MountGUI).

@endnode

@node Intro "Introduction"

  @{b}How it works@{ub}

  The a314 disk service on the Pi runs a small line-based TCP server (by
  default localhost:23890) that understands, one line each:

      insert <drive> <pi-path>       mount an image read-only
      insert <drive> -rw <pi-path>   mount writable
      eject  <drive>                 unmount

  <drive> is 0 to 3 (PD0: to PD3:). A314Mount reaches the server over
  @{b}bsdsocket.library@{ub} (provided by a314bsd), so the socket calls run on
  the Pi and 127.0.0.1 is the Pi. It opens a socket, sends one line, and
  reports the result.

  It is a front end for Niklas Ekstrom's a314 disk service; see the
  Software/disk directory of the a314 project.

@endnode

@node Reqs "Requirements"

  @{b}You need:@{ub}

  * A working bsdsocket.library - @{b}a314bsd@{ub} - so 127.0.0.1 routes to
    the Pi.

  * The a314 @{b}disk driver@{ub} installed: a314disk.device in DEVS: and the
    PD0: to PD3: drives mounted. See @{" Installation " link Install}.

  The daemon on :23890 is an @{b}on-demand@{ub} service - it only starts (and
  starts listening) once the Amiga opens a drive, so the drive must exist and
  have been accessed at least once. If nothing has touched a drive yet,
  A314Mount reports "Cannot reach the disk daemon".

@endnode

@node Install "Installation"

  Copy the A314Mount drawer onto the Amiga. Then either:

  * @{b}Double-click the Install icon@{ub} - it runs the install script via
    IconX and copies everything to SYS:Utilities and C:, or

  * From a Shell in the drawer:

        Execute Install               ; GUI to SYS:Utilities, CLI to C:
        Execute Install SYS:Tools     ; ...or name your own drawer

  The installer places @{b}A314MountGUI@{ub} + its icon in the chosen drawer
  (double-click to launch), copies the @{b}A314Mount@{ub} CLI to C:, and - if
  DEVS:a314disk.device is present - installs the PD0: to PD3: drive
  descriptors to DEVS:DOSDrivers/. After that, "Mount PD0:" (or a reboot)
  brings a drive up; the first access to it starts the Pi daemon.

@endnode

@node CLI "CLI usage"

      A314Mount INSERT [DRIVE n] <image> [RW] [HOST h] [PORT p] [SHOW]
      A314Mount EJECT  [DRIVE n]         [HOST h] [PORT p] [SHOW]

  @{b}INSERT / EJECT@{ub}  pick one.
  @{b}DRIVE@{ub}   virtual unit, 0 = PD0: (default) .. 3 = PD3:.
  @{b}<image>@{ub} Pi-side path of the ADF or HDF (e.g. /home/pi/wb.adf).
  @{b}RW@{ub}      mount writable (default read-only; usually wanted for HDF).
  @{b}HOST/PORT@{ub} daemon target (default 127.0.0.1 : 23890). HOST is a
            numeric IP.
  @{b}SHOW@{ub}    print the line that would be sent, don't send it (dry run).

  @{b}Examples@{ub}

      A314Mount INSERT /home/pi/Workbench.adf         ; PD0:, read-only
      A314Mount INSERT DRIVE 1 /home/pi/games.adf RW  ; PD1:, writable
      A314Mount INSERT DRIVE 2 /home/pi/work.hdf RW   ; an HDF on PD2:
      A314Mount EJECT                                 ; eject PD0:
      A314Mount EJECT DRIVE 1

@endnode

@node GUI "GUI usage"

  @{b}Double-click A314MountGUI@{ub} from Workbench, or run it from a Shell.

  Pick the @{b}Drive@{ub} (PD0: to PD3:), set the @{b}Image@{ub}, tick
  @{b}Read-write@{ub} if needed, and click @{b}Insert@{ub} or @{b}Eject@{ub}.
  @{b}Host@{ub} / @{b}Port@{ub} default to 127.0.0.1 / 23890. The status line
  shows the result, e.g. "PD0: inserted".

  Set the Image two ways: type the Pi-side path, or click the @{b}folder
  button@{ub} at the end of the Image row (clicking Insert with an empty field
  opens it too). That opens a built-in @{b}Pi directory browser@{ub} and shows
  the directory in a scrolling list (subdirectories first with a trailing "/",
  then .adf/.hdf/.adz files). Select an entry and @{b}Open@{ub} to descend into
  a directory or pick a file; @{b}Parent@{ub} goes up. Picking a file drops its
  absolute Pi path into the Image field - it can reach anywhere on the Pi you
  can read, not just the a314 share.

  It is fully standalone: it lists directories by running the stock @{b}pi@{ub}
  command (the a314 picmd service), so nothing on the Pi needs modifying.

@endnode

@node HDF "ADF vs HDF"

  Both ADF and HDF images go into the same PD0: to PD3: drives with the same
  INSERT / EJECT. The only difference is the @{b}mount-list geometry@{ub} the
  drive was created with:

  * @{b}ADF@{ub} - every floppy is 901120 bytes, so the stock PD0: to PD3:
    descriptors (floppy geometry) work for any ADF. Nothing extra to do.

  * @{b}HDF@{ub} - geometry is per-image (Surfaces=1, BlocksPerTrack=32,
    HighCyl = size/16384 - 1), and the Amiga mount entry @{b}must match the
    image@{ub} or the filesystem reads the wrong blocks. Generate a correct
    entry with the bundled helper (pure Python, runs on the Pi or the host):

        python3 mkhdfmount.py /home/pi/work.hdf 2 WORK  > WORK

    Copy the result to DEVS:DOSDrivers/WORK, "Mount WORK:", then
    "A314Mount INSERT DRIVE 2 /home/pi/work.hdf RW". Use a drive unit not
    already claimed by a floppy - a unit's geometry locks to the first image
    inserted after each reboot. HDFs are almost always mounted RW. For an
    FFS-formatted HDF, add the FileSystem/DosType lines the helper notes.

@endnode

@node Trouble "Troubleshooting"

  @{b}"Cannot reach the disk daemon (not listening?)"@{ub}
    The drive isn't up yet. Mount it (e.g. "Mount PD0:") and access it once
    (e.g. "dir PD0:") so a314disk.device connects and the Pi daemon starts.

  @{b}"No bsdsocket.library ..."@{ub}
    a314bsd (or a TCP stack) isn't running. Start it first.

  @{b}"Host must be a numeric IP"@{ub}
    Give HOST a dotted-quad address; names aren't resolved. The default
    127.0.0.1 is correct for a314bsd (socket ops run on the Pi).

  @{b}No "daemon:" reply line@{ub}
    Normal. a314bsd doesn't honour SO_RCVTIMEO, so A314Mount skips the reply
    read rather than risk hanging. The insert/eject still succeeds.

  @{b}HDF mounts but shows garbage / won't validate@{ub}
    The mount-list geometry doesn't match the image. Regenerate the entry
    with mkhdfmount.py; see @{" ADF vs HDF " link HDF}.

@endnode
